// =============================================================================
// world-market-course — "The World Market: Countries That Trade"
// =============================================================================
// Course THREE of the Stock Market department. Darrell 2026-09-19 named all
// four in one sentence: "Stock Market courses to explore and explain the world
// of stock and bonds and countries that trade and how investment works world
// wide." Course one
// answered STOCK, course two answered BONDS, and both were about an instrument
// held by a person. This one is about the system those instruments sit inside,
// and it answers the half of his sentence neither of the others touched:
// COUNTRIES THAT TRADE.
//
// THE ARC, one argument in eight moves:
//   1. Why anybody trades across a border at all   (Genesis 42:1, 2)
//   2. Currency: the price of one money in another (1 Kings 10:22)
//   3. A trade deficit is not a debt               (Ezekiel 27:33)
//   4. What still has to be dug, grown and shipped (Proverbs 31:14)
//   5. Who issues the money, and what a central bank actually does (Deuteronomy 8:17, 18)
//   6. Reserves, and why one country's money reaches a person who never leaves their state (Isaiah 23:8)
//   7. When the pipes get closed                   (Revelation 18:11, 17)
//   8. The just weight across a border             (Leviticus 19:35, 36)
//
// WHY LESSON 3 IS THE ONE THIS COURSE EXISTS FOR. "Trade deficit" is among the
// most confidently misused phrases in public life, and the confusion is not
// partisan -- it is arithmetic. A trade deficit is not a debt, nobody is owed
// anything at the end of a year of it, and a country running one is receiving
// goods and sending money, which is either a good bargain or a bad one
// depending on what the money was doing otherwise. Stating that plainly is not
// taking a side on trade policy and this course does not take one; it is
// refusing to let a reader be moved by a word they were never taught.
//
// THE LINE THIS COURSE HOLDS ON COMPARATIVE ADVANTAGE (DR-0100). The reason
// two countries both gain by trading is real, it is not controversial among
// people who have examined it, and it is stated plainly. AND the losses are
// stated just as plainly: a gain spread thinly across a country and a loss
// concentrated in one town are not the same experience, the aggregate being
// positive is no comfort to the town, and a course that gives the theorem
// without the town has told half the truth. Both halves, in the same breath,
// the way the Banking course handles fees.
//
// AND WHAT THIS COURSE REFUSES TO DO. It names no country as a villain and
// none as a model, it takes no position on any live trade dispute, and it
// quotes no figure -- balances, reserves and exchange rates move daily, and a
// number printed in a lesson is wrong by the time it is read.
//
// VERIFICATION (DR-0076 / SCRIPTURE-REFERENCE-STANDARD). Every quoted span is
// fetched VERBATIM from the repository's own KJV. Our prose says Yahweh;
// quotations keep "God" and "the LORD" exactly as the KJV has them (DR-0210).
//
// TEACHING, NOT FINANCIAL OR POLICY ADVICE -- carried on the meta.
// =============================================================================

