        kind: 'parable', tone: 'light',
        title: 'Neither Of Us Wants Yours',
        body: 'A man who imported machine parts tried for a year to persuade a supplier to invoice him in his own currency and could not understand the refusal, because the supplier was perfectly friendly about everything else. He eventually asked directly and got an answer he had not expected: the supplier said he would be happy to, except that he would then be holding a currency he could not spend on anything he needed, and he would have to convert it, and the conversion would cost him, and the cost would come back to the importer in the price anyway. So they could do it the complicated way or the simple way and the price would end up the same. The importer said the thing that stayed with him was the phrase the supplier used, which was that neither of us wants yours. He said he had spent a year assuming it was a policy, or a snub, or a bank rule, and it was arithmetic, and a five-minute conversation would have told him at any point in that year if he had thought to have it.',
