    lesson: 'This session concerns something that sounds remote from an ordinary household and is not remote at all. NATIONS HOLD ONE ANOTHER’S CURRENCIES IN QUANTITY, deliberately, and the reason emerges from an ordinary problem rather than from any design. Consider a Brazilian firm purchasing steel from a Korean one. The Brazilian party holds reais — a claim on Brazilian output, as the second session established — and the Korean party has no use whatever for a claim on Brazilian output. The Korean party wants won, which the Brazilian party does not hold. BOTH THEREFORE REACH FOR A THIRD CURRENCY WHICH BOTH ACCEPT, and the transaction is denominated in it. Multiply that by the whole of cross-border commerce and one currency comes to be used on an enormous scale far outside the borders of the country that issues it. THE ISSUING COUNTRY RECEIVES TWO SUBSTANTIAL ADVANTAGES, and this course states both plainly rather than darkly. THE FIRST IS CHEAPER BORROWING. Everybody holding reserves needs somewhere secure to hold them, and the natural place is that country’s government obligations. That produces a STANDING DEMAND for its debt — and by exactly the mechanism the Bonds course set out, standing demand means a higher price, and a higher price on a fixed promise means a lower rate. A nation that borrows more cheaply than it otherwise would services its debt at a lower cost to its own taxpayers, permanently, without anyone voting on it. THE SECOND IS REACH. Where most of the world’s payments clear through one country’s system, that country acquires the capacity to decide who may use it. That is a genuine power, it has been exercised, and it is the subject of the next session. NEITHER ADVANTAGE IS CONCEALED AND NEITHER REQUIRES A CONSPIRACY, and this course will not supply one. This is what happens when a convenience hardens into a standard — in the same way a keyboard layout or a recording format wins. Nobody chose it collectively; everybody found it easier to go along; and the incumbent benefits from the fact that changing is costly for everybody at once, which is what makes a standard a standard. NOR IS IT PERMANENT. Reserve positions have shifted before. They shift SLOWLY — over decades rather than years, and usually invisibly while it is happening — and historically they accelerate after the holder of the position does something that gives the rest of the world a reason to start quietly building an alternative, not out of principle but out of prudence. THAT PATTERN IS MORE USEFUL THAN ANY FORECAST, because it converts an unanswerable question into a checkable one: instead of asking when a position will change, ask whether anybody is currently building an alternative and what made them start. The first question has never had a reliable answer. The second has had one in every century anybody has examined. AND HERE IS WHY IT REACHES A PERSON WHO NEVER LEAVES THEIR OWN STATE. Things they buy were priced in that third currency somewhere along the chain, even where they never handle a single unit of it. When it moves, somebody in that chain absorbs the difference — and which party absorbs it is settled by who has the least room to refuse, which is the ability-to-wait point from the previous session, standing one step further up the chain. AND ISAIAH, DESCRIBING A TRADING CITY WHOSE COMMERCIAL POSITION HAD BECOME A SOCIAL STATION: "Who hath taken this counsel against Tyre, the crowning city, whose merchants are princes, whose traffickers are the honourable of the earth?" (Isaiah 23:8) READ WHERE THAT SENTENCE SITS. It sounds like a tribute and it is inside a burden pronounced AGAINST the city. That is exactly why it belongs in this lesson rather than as decoration: the position that made her merchants into princes was not a permanent grant, and the prophet is announcing that it can be taken.',
